      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>What Fans Say</h2>
        <p>
          Real fans, real reactions — what the darts world is saying about the Grand Slam right now. Quotes from r/Darts, X, Facebook and Instagram, updated as the tournament goes on.
        </p>
        <h3>The double nine-darter broke the internet</h3>
        <p>
          Michael van Gerwen and Tom Sykes both hit nine-darters in the same quarter-final, and fans called it the greatest darts match ever. The PDC clip passed 120,000 likes on Instagram, with fans calling Van Gerwen "the GOAT" and saying "he's back".
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "This Tom Sykes v MVG game is the perfect advert for darts — absolutely sensational."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan on social media, via SPORTbible</em></p>
        <blockquote>
          "This is the greatest darts match ever. You can keep your Littlers and Humphries. It's now Sykes v Van Gerwen."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan on social media, via SPORTbible</em></p>
        <blockquote>
          "MvG televised 9 darter, I can't spake."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan on social media, via The Sun</em></p>
        <p>
          Full story on our <a href="/tournament/grand-slam-double-nine-darter/">two nine-darters page</a>.
        </p>
        <h3>The GTA VI jokes</h3>
        <p>
          After Littler's 10-8 last-16 loss to Wattimena, fans joked he lost on purpose to free up time for GTA VI's release day.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "Fair play to Littler sacrificing a darts title so he can play GTA for five days straight."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan on X, via Scottish Sun</em></p>
        <h3>The sportsmanship split</h3>
        <p>
          Fans split two ways over Littler walking off quickly after the defeat. One side called him out; the other defended him. Both sides agree on the facts: a quick handshake, then off without applauding the crowd.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "Straight off stage again, can't hack losing at all, can he?"
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan on X, via GiveMeSport</em></p>
        <blockquote>
          "He's a generational talent, and he knows it. Anything less than winning is unacceptable to him."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fan defending Littler, via GiveMeSport</em></p>
        <h3>Wattimena deserves his flowers</h3>
        <p>
          While the debate raged about Littler, fans praised the winner: a 101.70 average, 10 maximums, and a career-best night on the biggest stage.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "Deserves his flowers tonight."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— fans on X and Facebook</em></p>
        <h3>Triple bull — what is the football equivalent?</h3>
        <p>
          After Littler's 150 checkout with three bullseyes, r/Darts debated the football equivalent. The top answer: a perfect hat-trick — left foot, right foot, header. Others said a Panenka penalty, or a goalkeeper scoring in the Premier League — about as rare, with only three triple bulls in ten years: Littler, Joyce and Whitlock.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "Golden Hat-trick - Left foot, right-foot, and a header."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— u/DangerousOpinion1523, r/Darts (144 upvotes)</em></p>
        <h3>Why does every crowd sing the same five chants?</h3>
        <p>
          A non-British fan asked why every darts crowd, in any country, sings the same chants. The best answer: chants need coordination, and darts crowds are different people at every event — most go once a year — so everyone falls back on the few chants the whole room knows. Top addition: "Don't forget Boring Boring Tables."
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          "So the only thing people can do is fall back on the chants that everyone in the room will know and that's normally the same few ones that have been popular for years."
        </blockquote>
        <p><em>— u/scouserontravels, r/Darts (37 upvotes)</em></p>
